#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110260 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29005c66-9f30-5862-926f-8a09320d404c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Slope-Intercept Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/id #uuid "4cc37ad7-b382-5b7e-bcda-92b2818c5650" :question/math-academy-id "e-14359" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $y= -\\dfrac{2}{5}x-3$?\n\n1. $y = 5x + 1$\n2. $y = \\dfrac{5}{2}x + 3$\n3. $y = -\\dfrac{x}{2} + 5$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1$.\n\nThe line\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{2}{5}x-3\n$$\n\nis in slope-intercept form, and its slope is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{5}{2}x + 3\n$$\n\nhas a slope of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{5}{2} = -1\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1$, we conclude that the line\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{5}{2}x + 3\n$$\n\nis perpendicular to the given line.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110261 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f3d7d62-686b-59c4-9ea1-695c6be3180b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Unknown Parameters Given Two Perpendicular Lines"}] :question/id #uuid "a53a3337-108b-5129-b203-ff70ce8d0abe" :question/math-academy-id "e-14362" :question/problem "The lines $y=2x+9$ and $3y+kx = 12$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1$.\n\n- The first line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=2x+9$ is $2$.\n- To find the slope of $3y+kx = 12$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n$3y + kx|= 12; 3y|=-kx + 12; y|=-\\frac{k}{3}x + 4$\nTherefore, the slope of the line $3y +kx = 12$ is $-\\dfrac{k}{3}$.\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot\\left(-\\dfrac{k}{3}\\right) = -1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110262 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6fee8d5-e1f7-599d-a74c-abc44d973065" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equations of Perpendicular Horizontal and Vertical Lines"}] :question/id #uuid "5eca5ef2-627f-5d32-8edb-88e223b11d71" :question/math-academy-id "e-14365" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $x=2$ and passes through the point $(3,4)$." :question/worked-solution "Since the line $x=2$ is a vertical line, a perpendicular line would need to be horizontal. So, the perpendicular line must take the form $y=k$ for some value of $k$.\n\nThe perpendicular line needs to go through the point $(3,4)$, which has a $y$-coordinate of $4$. So, the perpendicular line is $y=4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110269 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e732f270-1d6f-5ff8-8792-d9b329ea858d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Standard Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/id #uuid "b5470454-6a27-5eda-a323-74ce90259f4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14734" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $4x - 2y = 10$?\n\n1. $y = \\dfrac{1}{2}x + 3$\n2. $y = -4x + 1$\n3. $y = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1$.\n\nTo find the slope of the given line\n\n$$\n4x - 2y = 10\n$$\n\nwe convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 2y &= 10 \\\\\n- 2y &= -4x + 10 \\\\\ny &= 2x - 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $2$.\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5\n$$\n\nhas a slope of\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = -1\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1$, we conclude that the line\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5\n$$\n\nis perpendicular to the given line.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6fee8d5-e1f7-599d-a74c-abc44d973065" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equations of Perpendicular Horizontal and Vertical Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e849878e-c5f7-5c4e-b0e6-846d382cddea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243827} {:answer/id #uuid "686a4134-a5d4-54f7-a21c-c214992d3440" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243828} {:answer/id #uuid "3c4b605d-8904-5046-97e4-ffb889235f81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243829} {:answer/id #uuid "0ada92ec-b6e6-51c9-964e-02b1e5aa6f85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243830} {:answer/id #uuid "2dc3a39e-5a3b-5af7-a5c9-c8ddcdad2749" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "686a4134-a5d4-54f7-a21c-c214992d3440" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243828} :answer-field/id #uuid "4453588e-3f70-5d0f-b3b1-4521d9a4080a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88e2feea-9e2f-596d-a324-3c357fe60543" :question/math-academy-id "q-110140" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $x=2$ and passes through the point $(1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the line $x=2$ is a vertical line, a perpendicular line would need to be horizontal. So, the perpendicular line must take the form $y=k$ for some value of $k.$\n\nThe perpendicular line needs to go through the point $(1,4),$ which has a $y$-coordinate of $4.$ So, the perpendicular line is $y=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6fee8d5-e1f7-599d-a74c-abc44d973065" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equations of Perpendicular Horizontal and Vertical Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c82e392-e55c-5344-9386-dcfb535ac7bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243834} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c42920-e9fc-5532-9aa3-7ecd9554446c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243835} {:answer/id #uuid "0c816aa5-43d9-50c5-8f35-33ad3040ae11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243836} {:answer/id #uuid "35ce6038-44b2-541f-9932-6ac3539b5ccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243837} {:answer/id #uuid "47fb6175-b717-58fd-9de0-011f80755ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35ce6038-44b2-541f-9932-6ac3539b5ccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243837} :answer-field/id #uuid "a28909b0-a1f7-5304-a153-c6ff2f09b951" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe416b2a-0b6c-5dc8-9038-3cc95892668a" :question/math-academy-id "q-110142" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $y=3$ and passes through the point $(-4,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the line $y=3$ is a horizontal line, a perpendicular line would need to be vertical. So, the perpendicular line must take the form $x=k$ for some value of $k.$\n\nThe perpendicular line needs to go through the point $(-4,-3),$ which has an $x$-coordinate of $-4.$ So, the perpendicular line is $x=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6fee8d5-e1f7-599d-a74c-abc44d973065" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equations of Perpendicular Horizontal and Vertical Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cc3ffd9-a173-5b7d-80db-a31ee9a93e0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243841} {:answer/id #uuid "1ac5ae83-2766-5b5b-8988-bf861302a0fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243842} {:answer/id #uuid "3668e530-329e-53d3-8f39-56319e827a10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243843} {:answer/id #uuid "25d9b3db-ff0f-587a-8538-e20948df4257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243844} {:answer/id #uuid "ab324fc3-e27b-5356-a333-ae15144552d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ac5ae83-2766-5b5b-8988-bf861302a0fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243842} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd0985e1-9fcd-564e-bb51-a0d3db90b429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61e7c50a-2378-58db-9a40-6f3731e9651b" :question/math-academy-id "q-110143" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ and passes through the point $(3,7).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the line $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is a vertical line, a perpendicular line would need to be horizontal. So, the perpendicular line must take the form $y=k$ for some value of $k.$\n\nThe perpendicular line needs to go through the point $(3,7),$ which has a $y$-coordinate of $7.$ So, the perpendicular line is $y=7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29005c66-9f30-5862-926f-8a09320d404c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Slope-Intercept Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0baa7295-5a66-5540-acdc-2f580ff58ad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243848} {:answer/id #uuid "88c99edb-4ea7-5a08-a93b-2f6831d2d3d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243849} {:answer/id #uuid "9da6b1f3-1897-5cd8-b0f4-e5e047c80525" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243850} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa58530-1ff8-51fb-a0cf-b1ce0b841107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243851} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbcb199-f000-5f22-9822-6700103c7222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbbcb199-f000-5f22-9822-6700103c7222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243852} :answer-field/id #uuid "99d468eb-b67c-535f-a044-c88e0dcc04a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76c5ed08-0518-5a08-849f-4180cc6315fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-215888" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $y=-x+5?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nThe line $y=-x+5$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $-1.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=x+3$ has a slope of $1.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot 1=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=x+3$ is perpendicular to the given line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29005c66-9f30-5862-926f-8a09320d404c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Slope-Intercept Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7688e72-d922-592b-91bf-8da17faaf359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243855} {:answer/id #uuid "336c559d-12e9-5d41-a518-3be48a3474b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243856} {:answer/id #uuid "7fda33cf-703e-5154-a538-ec45393954ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243857} {:answer/id #uuid "aaaa5854-fce3-55b4-b7b9-83dcb82bc539" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243858} {:answer/id #uuid "542fe3ee-11c7-52fc-9bc9-64df371b415e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fda33cf-703e-5154-a538-ec45393954ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243857} :answer-field/id #uuid "561ee252-9807-54fb-b3da-9bc79e0807e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a8c135b-3546-5c2a-8d92-a2c1ed13facd" :question/math-academy-id "q-215890" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $y=x+\\frac{1}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nThe line $y=x+\\frac{1}{2}$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $1.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=-x+9$ has a slope of $-1.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n1\\cdot (-1)=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=-x+9$ is perpendicular to the given line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f3d7d62-686b-59c4-9ea1-695c6be3180b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Unknown Parameters Given Two Perpendicular Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d252f6a-a6ed-5b6f-839d-8757f218ea91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d252f6a-a6ed-5b6f-839d-8757f218ea91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243862} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fe24769-facb-561b-8907-799770c5d13a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b789b4e-b370-5ab0-ad38-332e88544678" :question/math-academy-id "q-215939" :question/problem "The lines $y=2x+1$ and $kx-10y=3$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\n- The first line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=2x+1$ is $2.$\n\n- To find the slope of $kx-10y=3,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx-10y & =3 \\\\ 10y & =kx-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{k}{10}x-\\frac{3}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line $kx-10y=3$ is $\\frac{k}{10}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n2\\cdot (\\frac{k}{10})=-1\\,⟹\\,k=-5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f3d7d62-686b-59c4-9ea1-695c6be3180b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Unknown Parameters Given Two Perpendicular Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bdb4b9f-e6e9-50b7-96d7-b2f9da7cadb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bdb4b9f-e6e9-50b7-96d7-b2f9da7cadb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243865} :answer-field/id #uuid "2feff926-444f-55b2-8a81-15eea93cb07d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dedc61d6-f231-5dad-bf68-62d6505aac80" :question/math-academy-id "q-215941" :question/problem "The lines $y=-5x+3$ and $3kx-5y=9$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\n- The first line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=-5x+3$ is $-5.$\n\n- To find the slope of $3kx-5y=9,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3kx-5y & =9 \\\\ 5y & =3kx-9 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3k}{5}x-\\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line $3kx-5y=9$ is $\\frac{3k}{5}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-5)\\cdot (\\frac{3k}{5})=-1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e732f270-1d6f-5ff8-8792-d9b329ea858d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Standard Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c1bc78d-e1cd-5c68-9cad-4cef1425025c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243868} {:answer/id #uuid "0ccb436d-05f7-54c7-baf2-c8248f5fbefe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243869} {:answer/id #uuid "c739c91d-86c2-56ab-b6ef-3e5c5142165c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-8x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243870} {:answer/id #uuid "1f13ede2-082f-5b19-8ecb-fbfe40e026d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{8}x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243871} {:answer/id #uuid "e1007cc1-c24f-575a-86ee-486678a222c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{8}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f13ede2-082f-5b19-8ecb-fbfe40e026d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{8}x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243871} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2a1c68d-8f5e-5fb7-866a-059b815ef7b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a445286-4f26-53de-8033-c6835281bec3" :question/math-academy-id "q-23618" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $8x-y=2?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $8x-y=2,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-y & =2 \\\\ -y & =-8x+2 \\\\ y & =8x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $8.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=-\\frac{1}{8}x+8$ has a slope of $-\\frac{1}{8}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n8\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8})=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=-\\frac{1}{8}x+8$ is perpendicular to the given line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e732f270-1d6f-5ff8-8792-d9b329ea858d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Standard Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "019ba887-4a52-5987-9fcb-584664974399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243875} {:answer/id #uuid "c00f2e1b-01f9-5c58-80b6-3e3553cea379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243876} {:answer/id #uuid "e722abc3-7f5b-54ee-b57a-6a41098dee5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243877} {:answer/id #uuid "2d926487-efb8-567c-9e44-b2845e13c598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243878} {:answer/id #uuid "eab42c97-e58b-517c-9588-481ae5a191fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c00f2e1b-01f9-5c58-80b6-3e3553cea379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243876} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0d8bad4-8387-53d0-998c-3bdcafb411a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd871762-b717-5e30-8b16-ad2baaddc39a" :question/math-academy-id "q-23621" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $x+3y=6?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $x+3y=6,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3y & =6 \\\\ 3y & =-x+6 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=3x$ has a slope of $3.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 3=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=3x$ is perpendicular to the given line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6fee8d5-e1f7-599d-a74c-abc44d973065" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equations of Perpendicular Horizontal and Vertical Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25d07bc5-9477-54c8-b208-15e9ed8ff587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243882} {:answer/id #uuid "b5493dce-6068-53ba-b904-9298a69517f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243883} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c56b7f-078b-53ed-b93e-6c03001a8b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243884} {:answer/id #uuid "3af76b70-7d5d-534b-b216-8bb4aeb9782d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243885} {:answer/id #uuid "819e6fd7-ef94-5903-99e0-bc2ccc186b8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3af76b70-7d5d-534b-b216-8bb4aeb9782d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243885} :answer-field/id #uuid "a98eb5c9-602f-575c-9dca-39e4feb9afeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "838362a6-7010-5550-a533-17395c107385" :question/math-academy-id "q-23628" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(1,-2)$ and is perpendicular to $x=3.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the line $x=3$ is a vertical line, a perpendicular line would need to be horizontal. So, the perpendicular line must take the form $y=k$ for some value of $k.$\n\nThe perpendicular line needs to go through the point $(1,-2),$ which has a $y$-coordinate of $-2.$ So, the perpendicular line is $y=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f3d7d62-686b-59c4-9ea1-695c6be3180b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Unknown Parameters Given Two Perpendicular Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1dda77b-2b32-5165-950d-9e0a52a7ac4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243889} {:answer/id #uuid "f90c9f04-7a4c-507c-ab06-ed072af01b89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243890} {:answer/id #uuid "88f59d34-c649-5a28-9146-a9ae12057a18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243891} {:answer/id #uuid "0d7e130e-0517-5fbf-a835-96bee6ce4183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243892} {:answer/id #uuid "488ae477-beaf-5cad-9466-5603ba5e3d16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f90c9f04-7a4c-507c-ab06-ed072af01b89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243890} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba305f4f-fa96-5b5e-9408-be3173914f8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce3661d2-3a80-55a2-be91-090cefc2fbdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-28328" :question/problem "Given that the lines $y=5qx+9$ and $y=-3x-8$ are perpendicular, what is the value of $q?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nBoth lines are slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. \n\n- The slope of the line $y=5qx+9$ is $5q.$\n\n- The slope of the line $y=-3x-8$ is $-3.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n5q\\cdot (-3)=-1\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{1}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e732f270-1d6f-5ff8-8792-d9b329ea858d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Standard Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "892c2c15-da1d-5ddf-a4e4-075f23a6ace5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 5x - 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108762} {:answer/id #uuid "d67c86a9-bc07-525f-a8f3-0eda7891bc11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{1}{5}x + 15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108763} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9c49e1-3b7c-5805-aef2-6bd755a7d14d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-x + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108764} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb0b61b-101a-5626-80f4-9a0fd998a33c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-5x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108765} {:answer/id #uuid "77b98843-90f1-50bd-a72f-744e03b6e785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{5}x - 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "892c2c15-da1d-5ddf-a4e4-075f23a6ace5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 5x - 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108762} :answer-field/id #uuid "44d318db-f5ac-536a-8061-3350c15b7175" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8ebc497-48a2-5b22-a14f-221db06dd5d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-44404" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $x + 5y = 10$?" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $x+5y=10,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5y & =10 \\\\ 5y & =-x+10 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{5}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=5x-4$ has a slope of $5.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot 5=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=5x-4$ is perpendicular to the given line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f3d7d62-686b-59c4-9ea1-695c6be3180b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Unknown Parameters Given Two Perpendicular Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0774c27a-c36e-53ec-bd96-d01ba66699e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243896} {:answer/id #uuid "98db34bb-d034-58bc-8046-fd201e22300d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243897} {:answer/id #uuid "12dc05e7-1780-5094-b59e-0f2063438ecd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243898} {:answer/id #uuid "563ac7fd-1f06-5355-b267-40dadd1c8538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243899} {:answer/id #uuid "0e929f4a-f1bb-5ab4-83e5-d24448ccd926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e929f4a-f1bb-5ab4-83e5-d24448ccd926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243900} :answer-field/id #uuid "86ed7e8c-a30e-5b3f-9e79-44d994e1a548" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b7b2fbae-964a-51a8-a3e9-de9e38e6e854" :question/math-academy-id "q-44407" :question/problem "The lines $4x-8y=7$ and $3y-px=9$ are perpendicular. The value of $p=$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of $4x-8y=7,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-8y & =7 \\\\ -8y & =-4x+7 \\\\ 8y & =4x-7 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of $3y-px=9,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y-px & =9 \\\\ 3y & =px+9 \\\\ y & =\\frac{p}{3}x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The slope of the line $4x-8y=7$ is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of the line $3y-px=9$ is $\\frac{p}{3}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{p}{3}=-1\\,⟹\\,p=-6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e732f270-1d6f-5ff8-8792-d9b329ea858d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Standard Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cabb86d-a151-58d0-84be-85758aaf5dd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 3x + 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108768} {:answer/id #uuid "2d059998-937c-5a19-ab84-1142b1d54688" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-3x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108769} {:answer/id #uuid "43e6be3a-90f3-5ce7-800a-5b41dd3fabc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{1}{3}x + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108770} {:answer/id #uuid "671c4361-05df-572b-9454-c8c9da70ebdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108771} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea7ad26-9144-5afc-849d-e21c0aa21f36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{4}{5}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43e6be3a-90f3-5ce7-800a-5b41dd3fabc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{1}{3}x + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108770} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b3784b4-8208-5f87-8623-3c592c69f666" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7485dae-88b3-5eea-a431-272c46bd98d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-44514" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $12x - 4y = 5$?" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $12x-4y=5,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-4y & =5 \\\\ -4y & =-12x+5 \\\\ y & =3x-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $3.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=-\\frac{1}{3}x+3$ has a slope of $-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=-\\frac{1}{3}x+3$ is perpendicular to the given line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243901 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f3d7d62-686b-59c4-9ea1-695c6be3180b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Unknown Parameters Given Two Perpendicular Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c7a120a-580a-52f3-acf2-eba54fdc98a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243903} {:answer/id #uuid "3d24eb7f-d906-5b33-a5e2-7b40d57ee98d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243904} {:answer/id #uuid "20264605-d66a-5fed-805c-4e4edb703d67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243905} {:answer/id #uuid "62f21059-1c65-5150-8b66-0665bf58f74e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243906} {:answer/id #uuid "ee347f84-981b-5db4-9abd-c1a9975519b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c7a120a-580a-52f3-acf2-eba54fdc98a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243903} :answer-field/id #uuid "55c95b86-c5b8-55c9-ab7e-bc15fc7a136b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cd0156a4-c5d5-5aba-b4fb-5121463b4462" :question/math-academy-id "q-44515" :question/problem "The lines $15x+5y=9$ and $4y+3px=0$ are perpendicular. The value of $p=$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of $15x+5y=9,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15x+5y & =9 \\\\ 5y & =-15x+9 \\\\ y & =-3x+\\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of $4y+3px=0,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y+3px & =0 \\\\ 4y & =-3px \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3p}{4}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The slope of the line $15x+5y=9$ is $-3.$\n\n- The slope of the line $4y+3px=0$ is $-\\frac{3p}{4}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-3)\\cdot -\\frac{3p}{4}=-1\\,⟹\\,p=-\\frac{4}{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6fee8d5-e1f7-599d-a74c-abc44d973065" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equations of Perpendicular Horizontal and Vertical Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fae949e7-e71a-59a1-a4bc-7041848b354e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243910} {:answer/id #uuid "e312f7d3-95c1-5bd6-a840-f2734e4f353c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243911} {:answer/id #uuid "2b24d122-8b1e-54cb-9bd1-10ae4974e33b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243912} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb56cbf-9534-547c-b375-ccb1aaf61ec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243913} {:answer/id #uuid "e75c8d8d-4bac-5008-9c44-3777f0ef8888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b24d122-8b1e-54cb-9bd1-10ae4974e33b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243912} :answer-field/id #uuid "88fc00c5-1197-5c90-b229-262bddfa16b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02239b68-d660-5801-9ead-8e7f559793f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-44691" :question/problem "Determine the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-7,-9)$ and is perpendicular to $y=5.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the line $y=5$ is a horizontal line, a perpendicular line would need to be vertical. So, the perpendicular line must take the form $x=k$ for some value of $k.$\n\nThe perpendicular line needs to go through the point $(-7,-9),$ which has an $x$-coordinate of $-7.$ So, the perpendicular line is $x=-7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243915 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e732f270-1d6f-5ff8-8792-d9b329ea858d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Standard Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8056b55-bf4f-56b1-8714-902c3a23a9a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243917} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4a9087-5733-5704-931e-6c8283f22530" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243918} {:answer/id #uuid "bc77c2f3-14a8-58d8-97f8-c8ec1c068095" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243919} {:answer/id #uuid "0704705c-f701-5e9e-848d-012eeb8fa1b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}x-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243920} {:answer/id #uuid "57940993-3ab1-59d3-af05-10f54fa0fb1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57940993-3ab1-59d3-af05-10f54fa0fb1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243921} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6bd3f21-fdc6-5b29-8b89-b8bbabc51ff7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7df21e4b-12cf-5842-bae2-b10ce1dbd5d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-59763" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $4x+2y=8?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $4x+2y=8,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+2y & =8 \\\\ 2y & =-4x+8 \\\\ y & =-2x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-2.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+5$ has a slope of $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+5$ is perpendicular to the given line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29005c66-9f30-5862-926f-8a09320d404c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Slope-Intercept Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef2b93d7-0036-5099-853f-a69da6437feb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243924} {:answer/id #uuid "8a2e581c-4b90-5956-915f-73b3665fd911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243925} {:answer/id #uuid "edd37f4f-34cf-522a-ad44-6ac435d82980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243926} {:answer/id #uuid "05c1c39f-dcf4-5a73-812f-6620edf83def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243927} {:answer/id #uuid "157724cd-8632-5718-9979-7dd1e56872b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef2b93d7-0036-5099-853f-a69da6437feb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243924} :answer-field/id #uuid "df13c064-18c8-51d4-9d48-bcb685979369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0808e7d-6ff4-5fc1-ac7c-c42f5f392401" :question/math-academy-id "q-72524" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{x}{2}+7?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nThe line $y=-\\frac{x}{2}+7$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=2x+19$ has a slope of $2.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=2x+19$ is perpendicular to the given line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29005c66-9f30-5862-926f-8a09320d404c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Slope-Intercept Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "999d1316-34f7-566d-bf0f-bbbb5d7a3dc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}x-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243931} {:answer/id #uuid "21d693a8-c8f7-5612-a2a5-dbf0b40aadfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{4}x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243932} {:answer/id #uuid "5b44eed8-d183-5ea8-9725-8d15355f801f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243933} {:answer/id #uuid "f92676ae-d2b3-5e0c-b8ed-c84bed4e3525" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243934} {:answer/id #uuid "88d77084-86e3-5430-ba52-679ec0e65640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 175921862